    l1_reg_H : float, default=0.\n459 |         L1 regularization parameter for H.\n460 | \n461 |     l2_reg_W : float, default=0.\n462 |         L2 regularization parameter for W.\n463 | \n464 |     l2_reg_H : float, default=0.\n465 |         L2 regularization parameter for H.\n466 | \n467 |     update_H : bool, default=True\n468 |         Set to True, both W and H will be estimated from initial guesses.\n469 |         Set to False, only W will be estimated.\n470 | \n471 |     verbose : int, default=0\n472 |         The verbosity level.\n473 | \n474 |     shuffle : bool, default=False\n475 |         If true, randomize the order of coordinates in the CD solver.\n476 | \n477 |     random_state : int, RandomState instance or None, default=None\n478 |         Used to randomize the coordinates in the CD solver, when\n479 |         ``shuffle`` is set to ``True``. Pass an int for reproducible\n480 |         results across multiple function calls.\n481 |         See :term:`Glossary <random_state>`.\n482 | \n483 |     Returns\n484 |     -------\n485 |     W : ndarray of shape (n_samples, n_components)\n486 |         Solution to the non-negative least squares problem.\n487 | \n488 |     H : ndarray of shape (n_components, n_features)\n489 |         Solution to the non-negative least squares problem.\n490 | \n491 |     n_iter : int\n492 |         The number of iterations done by the algorithm.\n493 | \n494 |     References\n495 |     ----------\n496 |     Cichocki, Andrzej, and Phan, Anh-Huy. \"Fast local algorithms for\n497 |     large scale nonnegative matrix and tensor factorizations.\"\n498 |     IEICE transactions on fundamentals of electronics, communications and\n499 |     computer sciences 92.3: 708-721, 2009.\n500 |     \"\"\"\n501 |     # so W and Ht are both in C order in memory\n502 |     Ht = check_array(H.T, order=\"C\")\n503 |     X = check_array(X, accept_sparse=\"csr\")\n504 | \n505 |     rng = check_random_state(random_state)\n506 | \n507 |     for n_iter in range(1, max_iter + 1):\n508 |         violation = 0.0\n509 | \n510 |         # Update W\n511 |         violation += _update_coordinate_descent(\n512 |             X, W, Ht, l1_reg_W, l2_reg_W, shuffle, rng\n513 |         )\n514 |         # Update H\n515 |         if update_H:\n516 |             violation += _update_coordinate_descent(\n517 |                 X.T, Ht, W, l1_reg_H, l2_reg_H, shuffle, rng\n518 |             )\n519 | \n520 |         if n_iter == 1:\n521 |             violation_init = violation\n522 | \n523 |         if violation_init == 0:\n524 |             break\n525 | \n526 |         if verbose:\n527 |             print(\"violation:\", violation / violation_init)\n528 | \n529 |         if violation / violation_init <= tol:\n530 |             if verbose:\n531 |                 print(\"Converged at iteration\", n_iter + 1)\n532 |             break\n533 | \n534 |     return W, Ht.T, n_iter\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/feature_extraction/text.py:HashingVectorizer.fit`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n784 | def fit(self, X, y=None):\n785 |         \"\"\"No-op: this transformer is stateless.\n786 | \n787 |         Parameters\n788 |         ----------\n789 |         X : ndarray of shape [n_samples, n_features]\n790 |             Training data.\n791 | \n792 |         y : Ignored\n793 |             Not used, present for API consistency by convention.\n794 | \n795 |         Returns\n796 |         -------\n797 |         self : object\n798 |             HashingVectorizer instance.\n799 |         \"\"\"\n800 |         # triggers a parameter validation\n801 |         if isinstance(X, str):\n802 |             raise ValueError(\n803 |                 \"Iterable over raw text documents expected, string object received.\"\n804 |             )\n805 | \n806 |         self._warn_for_unused_params()\n807 |         self._validate_params()\n808 | \n809 |         self._get_hasher().fit(X, y=y)\n810 |         return self\n```\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"Non-negative Matrix Factorization\", \"MiniBatchNMF Non-negative Matrix Factorization beta divergence multiplicative updates\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `sklearn/decomposition/_nmf.py:non_negative_factorization`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n 874 | def non_negative_factorization(\n 875 |     X,\n 876 |     W=None,\n 877 |     H=None,\n 878 |     n_components=None,\n 879 |     *,\n 880 |     init=None,\n 881 |     update_H=True,\n 882 |     solver=\"cd\",\n 883 |     beta_loss=\"frobenius\",\n 884 |     tol=1e-4,\n 885 |     max_iter=200,\n 886 |     alpha=\"deprecated\",\n 887 |     alpha_W=0.0,\n 888 |     alpha_H=\"same\",\n 889 |     l1_ratio=0.0,\n 890 |     regularization=\"deprecated\",\n 891 |     random_state=None,\n 892 |     verbose=0,\n 893 |     shuffle=False,\n 894 | ):\n 895 |     \"\"\"Compute Non-negative Matrix Factorization (NMF).\n 896 | \n 897 |     Find two non-negative matrices (W, H) whose product approximates the non-\n 898 |     negative matrix X. This factorization can be used for example for\n 899 |     dimensionality reduction, source separation or topic extraction.\n 900 | \n 901 |     The objective function is:\n 902 | \n 903 |         .. math::\n 904 | \n 905 |             0.5 * ||X - WH||_{loss}^2\n 906 | \n 907 |             + alpha\\\\_W * l1_{ratio} * n\\\\_features * ||vec(W)||_1\n 908 | \n 909 |             + alpha\\\\_H * l1_{ratio} * n\\\\_samples * ||vec(H)||_1\n 910 | \n 911 |             + 0.5 * alpha\\\\_W * (1 - l1_{ratio}) * n\\\\_features * ||W||_{Fro}^2\n 912 | \n 913 |             + 0.5 * alpha\\\\_H * (1 - l1_{ratio}) * n\\\\_samples * ||H||_{Fro}^2\n 914 | \n 915 |     Where:\n 916 | \n 917 |     :math:`||A||_{Fro}^2 = \\\\sum_{i,j} A_{ij}^2` (Frobenius norm)\n 918 | \n 919 |     :math:`||vec(A)||_1 = \\\\sum_{i,j} abs(A_{ij})` (Elementwise L1 norm)\n 920 | \n 921 |     The generic norm :math:`||X - WH||_{loss}^2` may represent\n 922 |     the Frobenius norm or another supported beta-divergence loss.\n 923 |     The choice between options is controlled by the `beta_loss` parameter.\n 924 | \n 925 |     The regularization terms are scaled by `n_features` for `W` and by `n_samples` for\n 926 |     `H` to keep their impact balanced with respect to one another and to the data fit\n 927 |     term as independent as possible of the size `n_samples` of the training set.\n 928 | \n 929 |     The objective function is minimized with an alternating minimization of W\n 930 |     and H. If H is given and update_H=False, it solves for W only.\n 931 | \n 932 |     Note that the transformed data is named W and the components matrix is named H. In\n 933 |     the NMF literature, the naming convention is usually the opposite since the data\n 934 |     matrix X is transposed.\n 935 | \n 936 |     Parameters\n 937 |     ----------\n 938 |     X : array-like of shape (n_samples, n_features)\n 939 |         Constant matrix.\n 940 | \n 941 |     W : array-like of shape (n_samples, n_components), default=None\n 942 |         If init='custom', it is used as initial guess for the solution.\n 943 | \n 944 |     H : array-like of shape (n_components, n_features), default=None\n 945 |         If init='custom', it is used as initial guess for the solution.\n 946 |         If update_H=False, it is used as a constant, to solve for W only.\n 947 | \n 948 |     n_components : int, default=None\n 949 |         Number of components, if n_components is not set all features\n 950 |         are kept.\n 951 | \n 952 |     init : {'random', 'nndsvd', 'nndsvda', 'nndsvdar', 'custom'}, default=None\n 953 |         Method used to initialize the procedure.\n 954 | \n 955 |         Valid options:\n 956 | \n 957 |         - None: 'nndsvda' if n_components < n_features, otherwise 'random'.\n 958 | \n 959 |         - 'random': non-negative random matrices, scaled with:\n 960 |             sqrt(X.mean() / n_components)\n 961 | \n 962 |         - 'nndsvd': Nonnegative Double Singular Value Decomposition (NNDSVD)\n 963 |             initialization (better for sparseness)\n 964 | \n 965 |         - 'nndsvda': NNDSVD with zeros filled with the average of X\n 966 |             (better when sparsity is not desired)\n 967 | \n 968 |         - 'nndsvdar': NNDSVD with zeros filled with small random values\n 969 |             (generally faster, less accurate alternative to NNDSVDa\n 970 |             for when sparsity is not desired)\n 971 | \n 972 |         - 'custom': use custom matrices W and H if `update_H=True`. If\n 973 |           `update_H=False`, then only custom matrix H is used.\n 974 | \n 975 |         .. versionchanged:: 0.23\n 976 |             The default value of `init` changed from 'random' to None in 0.23.\n 977 | \n 978 |         .. versionchanged:: 1.1\n 979 |             When `init=None` and n_components is less than n_samples and n_features\n 980 |             defaults to `nndsvda` instead of `nndsvd`.\n 981 | \n 982 |     update_H : bool, default=True\n 983 |         Set to True, both W and H will be estimated from initial guesses.\n 984 |         Set to False, only W will be estimated.\n 985 | \n 986 |     solver : {'cd', 'mu'}, default='cd'\n 987 |         Numerical solver to use:\n 988 | \n 989 |         - 'cd' is a Coordinate Descent solver that uses Fast Hierarchical\n 990 |             Alternating Least Squares (Fast HALS).\n 991 | \n 992 |         - 'mu' is a Multiplicative Update solver.\n 993 | \n 994 |         .. versionadded:: 0.17\n 995 |            Coordinate Descent solver.\n 996 | \n 997 |         .. versionadded:: 0.19\n 998 |            Multiplicative Update solver.\n 999 | \n1000 |     beta_loss : float or {'frobenius', 'kullback-leibler', \\\n1001 |             'itakura-saito'}, default='frobenius'\n1002 |         Beta divergence to be minimized, measuring the distance between X\n1003 |         and the dot product WH. Note that values different from 'frobenius'\n1004 |         (or 2) and 'kullback-leibler' (or 1) lead to significantly slower\n1005 |         fits. Note that for beta_loss <= 0 (or 'itakura-saito'), the input\n1006 |         matrix X cannot contain zeros. Used only in 'mu' solver.\n1007 | \n1008 |         .. versionadded:: 0.19\n1009 | \n1010 |     tol : float, default=1e-4\n1011 |         Tolerance of the stopping condition.\n1012 | \n1013 |     max_iter : int, default=200\n1014 |         Maximum number of iterations before timing out.\n1015 | \n1016 |     alpha : float, default=0.0\n1017 |         Constant that multiplies the regularization terms. Set it to zero to have no\n1018 |         regularization. When using `alpha` instead of `alpha_W` and `alpha_H`, the\n1019 |         regularization terms are not scaled by the `n_features` (resp. `n_samples`)\n1020 |         factors for `W` (resp. `H`).\n1021 | \n1022 |         .. deprecated:: 1.0\n1023 |             The `alpha` parameter is deprecated in 1.0 and will be removed in 1.2.\n1024 |             Use `alpha_W` and `alpha_H` instead.\n1025 | \n1026 |     alpha_W : float, default=0.0\n1027 |         Constant that multiplies the regularization terms of `W`. Set it to zero\n1028 |         (default) to have no regularization on `W`.\n1029 | \n1030 |         .. versionadded:: 1.0\n1031 | \n1032 |     alpha_H : float or \"same\", default=\"same\"\n1033 |         Constant that multiplies the regularization terms of `H`. Set it to zero to\n1034 |         have no regularization on `H`. If \"same\" (default), it takes the same value as\n1035 |         `alpha_W`.\n1036 | \n1037 |         .. versionadded:: 1.0\n1038 | \n1039 |     l1_ratio : float, default=0.0\n1040 |         The regularization mixing parameter, with 0 <= l1_ratio <= 1.\n1041 |         For l1_ratio = 0 the penalty is an elementwise L2 penalty\n1042 |         (aka Frobenius Norm).\n1043 |         For l1_ratio = 1 it is an elementwise L1 penalty.\n1044 |         For 0 < l1_ratio < 1, the penalty is a combination of L1 and L2.\n1045 | \n1046 |     regularization : {'both', 'components', 'transformation'}, default=None\n1047 |         Select whether the regularization affects the components (H), the\n1048 |         transformation (W), both or none of them.\n1049 | \n1050 |         .. deprecated:: 1.0\n1051 |             The `regularization` parameter is deprecated in 1.0 and will be removed in\n1052 |             1.2. Use `alpha_W` and `alpha_H` instead.\n1053 | \n1054 |     random_state : int, RandomState instance or None, default=None\n1055 |         Used for NMF initialisation (when ``init`` == 'nndsvdar' or\n1056 |         'random'), and in Coordinate Descent. Pass an int for reproducible\n1057 |         results across multiple function calls.\n1058 |         See :term:`Glossary <random_state>`.\n1059 | \n1060 |     verbose : int, default=0\n1061 |         The verbosity level.\n1062 | \n1063 |     shuffle : bool, default=False\n1064 |         If true, randomize the order of coordinates in the CD solver.\n1065 | \n1066 |     Returns\n1067 |     -------\n1068 |     W : ndarray of shape (n_samples, n_components)\n1069 |         Solution to the non-negative least squares problem.\n1070 | \n1071 |     H : ndarray of shape (n_components, n_features)\n1072 |         Solution to the non-negative least squares problem.\n1073 | \n1074 |     n_iter : int\n1075 |         Actual number of iterations.\n1076 | \n1077 |     Examples\n1078 |     --------\n1079 |     >>> import numpy as np\n1080 |     >>> X = np.array([[1,1], [2, 1], [3, 1.2], [4, 1], [5, 0.8], [6, 1]])\n1081 |     >>> from sklearn.decomposition import non_negative_factorization\n1082 |     >>> W, H, n_iter = non_negative_factorization(X, n_components=2,\n1083 |     ... init='random', random_state=0)\n1084 | \n1085 |     References\n1086 |     ----------\n1087 |     Cichocki, Andrzej, and P. H. A. N. Anh-Huy. \"Fast local algorithms for\n1088 |     large scale nonnegative matrix and tensor factorizations.\"\n1089 |     IEICE transactions on fundamentals of electronics, communications and\n1090 |     computer sciences 92.3: 708-721, 2009.\n1091 | \n1092 |     Fevotte, C., & Idier, J. (2011). Algorithms for nonnegative matrix\n1093 |     factorization with the beta-divergence. Neural Computation, 23(9).\n1094 |     \"\"\"\n1095 |     X = check_array(X, accept_sparse=(\"csr\", \"csc\"), dtype=[np.float64, np.float32])\n1096 | \n1097 |     est = NMF(\n1098 |         n_components=n_components,\n1099 |         init=init,\n1100 |         solver=solver,\n1101 |         beta_loss=beta_loss,\n1102 |         tol=tol,\n1103 |         max_iter=max_iter,\n1104 |         random_state=random_state,\n1105 |         alpha=alpha,\n1106 |         alpha_W=alpha_W,\n1107 |         alpha_H=alpha_H,\n1108 |         l1_ratio=l1_ratio,\n1109 |         verbose=verbose,\n1110 |         shuffle=shuffle,\n1111 |         regularization=regularization,\n1112 |     )\n1113 | \n1114 |     with config_context(assume_finite=True):\n1115 |         W, H, n_iter = est._fit_transform(X, W=W, H=H, update_H=update_H)\n1116 | \n1117 |     return W, H, n_iter\n```\n\nFound function `benchmarks/bench_plot_nmf.py:_nls_subproblem`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n 49 | def _nls_subproblem(\n 50 |     X, W, H, tol, max_iter, alpha=0.0, l1_ratio=0.0, sigma=0.01, beta=0.1\n 51 | ):\n 52 |     \"\"\"Non-negative least square solver\n 53 |     Solves a non-negative least squares subproblem using the projected\n 54 |     gradient descent algorithm.\n 55 |     Parameters\n 56 |     ----------\n 57 |     X : array-like, shape (n_samples, n_features)\n 58 |         Constant matrix.\n 59 |     W : array-like, shape (n_samples, n_components)\n 60 |         Constant matrix.\n 61 |     H : array-like, shape (n_components, n_features)\n 62 |         Initial guess for the solution.\n 63 |     tol : float\n 64 |         Tolerance of the stopping condition.\n 65 |     max_iter : int\n 66 |         Maximum number of iterations before timing out.\n 67 |     alpha : double, default: 0.\n 68 |         Constant that multiplies the regularization terms. Set it to zero to\n 69 |         have no regularization.\n 70 |     l1_ratio : double, default: 0.\n 71 |         The regularization mixing parameter, with 0 <= l1_ratio <= 1.\n 72 |         For l1_ratio = 0 the penalty is an L2 penalty.\n 73 |         For l1_ratio = 1 it is an L1 penalty.\n 74 |         For 0 < l1_ratio < 1, the penalty is a combination of L1 and L2.\n 75 |     sigma : float\n 76 |         Constant used in the sufficient decrease condition checked by the line\n 77 |         search.  Smaller values lead to a looser sufficient decrease condition,\n 78 |         thus reducing the time taken by the line search, but potentially\n 79 |         increasing the number of iterations of the projected gradient\n 80 |         procedure. 0.01 is a commonly used value in the optimization\n 81 |         literature.\n 82 |     beta : float\n 83 |         Factor by which the step size is decreased (resp. increased) until\n 84 |         (resp. as long as) the sufficient decrease condition is satisfied.\n 85 |         Larger values allow to find a better step size but lead to longer line\n 86 |         search. 0.1 is a commonly used value in the optimization literature.\n 87 |     Returns\n 88 |     -------\n 89 |     H : array-like, shape (n_components, n_features)\n 90 |         Solution to the non-negative least squares problem.\n 91 |     grad : array-like, shape (n_components, n_features)\n 92 |         The gradient.\n 93 |     n_iter : int\n 94 |         The number of iterations done by the algorithm.\n 95 |     References\n 96 |     ----------\n 97 |     C.-J. Lin. Projected gradient methods for non-negative matrix\n 98 |     factorization. Neural Computation, 19(2007), 2756-2779.\n 99 |     https://www.csie.ntu.edu.tw/~cjlin/nmf/\n100 |     \"\"\"\n101 |     WtX = safe_sparse_dot(W.T, X)\n102 |     WtW = np.dot(W.T, W)\n103 | \n104 |     # values justified in the paper (alpha is renamed gamma)\n105 |     gamma = 1\n106 |     for n_iter in range(1, max_iter + 1):\n107 |         grad = np.dot(WtW, H) - WtX\n108 |         if alpha > 0 and l1_ratio == 1.0:\n109 |             grad += alpha\n110 |         elif alpha > 0:\n111 |             grad += alpha * (l1_ratio + (1 - l1_ratio) * H)\n112 | \n113 |         # The following multiplication with a boolean array is more than twice\n114 |         # as fast as indexing into grad.\n115 |         if _norm(grad * np.logical_or(grad < 0, H > 0)) < tol:\n116 |             break\n117 | \n118 |         Hp = H\n119 | \n120 |         for inner_iter in range(20):\n121 |             # Gradient step.\n122 |             Hn = H - gamma * grad\n123 |             # Projection step.\n124 |             Hn *= Hn > 0\n125 |             d = Hn - H\n126 |             gradd = np.dot(grad.ravel(), d.ravel())\n127 |             dQd = np.dot(np.dot(WtW, d).ravel(), d.ravel())\n128 |             suff_decr = (1 - sigma) * gradd + 0.5 * dQd < 0\n129 |             if inner_iter == 0:\n130 |                 decr_gamma = not suff_decr\n131 | \n132 |             if decr_gamma:\n133 |                 if suff_decr:\n134 |                     H = Hn\n135 |                     break\n136 |                 else:\n137 |                     gamma *= beta\n138 |             elif not suff_decr or (Hp == Hn).all():\n139 |                 H = Hp\n140 |                 break\n141 |             else:\n142 |                 gamma /= beta\n143 |                 Hp = Hn\n144 | \n145 |     if n_iter == max_iter:\n146 |         warnings.warn(\"Iteration limit reached in nls subproblem.\", ConvergenceWarning)\n147 | \n148 |     return H, grad, n_iter\n```\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"Non-negative Matrix Factorization\", \"beta divergence\", \"MiniBatchNMF Non-negative Matrix Factorization beta divergence multiplicative updates\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `sklearn/decomposition/_nmf.py:_fit_multiplicative_update`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n725 | def _fit_multiplicative_update(\n726 |     X,\n727 |     W,\n728 |     H,\n729 |     beta_loss=\"frobenius\",\n730 |     max_iter=200,\n731 |     tol=1e-4,\n732 |     l1_reg_W=0,\n733 |     l1_reg_H=0,\n734 |     l2_reg_W=0,\n735 |     l2_reg_H=0,\n736 |     update_H=True,\n737 |     verbose=0,\n738 | ):\n739 |     \"\"\"Compute Non-negative Matrix Factorization with Multiplicative Update.\n740 | \n741 |     The objective function is _beta_divergence(X, WH) and is minimized with an\n742 |     alternating minimization of W and H. Each minimization is done with a\n743 |     Multiplicative Update.\n744 | \n745 |     Parameters\n746 |     ----------\n747 |     X : array-like of shape (n_samples, n_features)\n748 |         Constant input matrix.\n749 | \n750 |     W : array-like of shape (n_samples, n_components)\n751 |         Initial guess for the solution.\n752 | \n753 |     H : array-like of shape (n_components, n_features)\n754 |         Initial guess for the solution.\n755 | \n756 |     beta_loss : float or {'frobenius', 'kullback-leibler', \\\n757 |             'itakura-saito'}, default='frobenius'\n758 |         String must be in {'frobenius', 'kullback-leibler', 'itakura-saito'}.\n759 |         Beta divergence to be minimized, measuring the distance between X\n760 |         and the dot product WH. Note that values different from 'frobenius'\n761 |         (or 2) and 'kullback-leibler' (or 1) lead to significantly slower\n762 |         fits. Note that for beta_loss <= 0 (or 'itakura-saito'), the input\n763 |         matrix X cannot contain zeros.\n764 | \n765 |     max_iter : int, default=200\n766 |         Number of iterations.\n767 | \n768 |     tol : float, default=1e-4\n769 |         Tolerance of the stopping condition.\n770 | \n771 |     l1_reg_W : float, default=0.\n772 |         L1 regularization parameter for W.\n773 | \n774 |     l1_reg_H : float, default=0.\n775 |         L1