te number or ``MAY_SHARE_BOUNDS``.\n1287 |        If in doubt, use `numpy.may_share_memory` instead.\n1288 | \n1289 |     Parameters\n1290 |     ----------\n1291 |     a, b : ndarray\n1292 |         Input arrays\n1293 |     max_work : int, optional\n1294 |         Effort to spend on solving the overlap problem (maximum number\n1295 |         of candidate solutions to consider). The following special\n1296 |         values are recognized:\n1297 | \n1298 |         max_work=MAY_SHARE_EXACT  (default)\n1299 |             The problem is solved exactly. In this case, the function returns\n1300 |             True only if there is an element shared between the arrays. Finding\n1301 |             the exact solution may take extremely long in some cases.\n1302 |         max_work=MAY_SHARE_BOUNDS\n1303 |             Only the memory bounds of a and b are checked.\n1304 | \n1305 |     Raises\n1306 |     ------\n1307 |     numpy.TooHardError\n1308 |         Exceeded max_work.\n1309 | \n1310 |     Returns\n1311 |     -------\n1312 |     out : bool\n1313 | \n1314 |     See Also\n1315 |     --------\n1316 |     may_share_memory\n1317 | \n1318 |     Examples\n1319 |     --------\n1320 |     >>> x = np.array([1, 2, 3, 4])\n1321 |     >>> np.shares_memory(x, np.array([5, 6, 7]))\n1322 |     False\n1323 |     >>> np.shares_memory(x[::2], x)\n1324 |     True\n1325 |     >>> np.shares_memory(x[::2], x[1::2])\n1326 |     False\n1327 | \n1328 |     Checking whether two arrays share memory is NP-complete, and\n1329 |     runtime may increase exponentially in the number of\n1330 |     dimensions. Hence, `max_work` should generally be set to a finite\n1331 |     number, as it is possible to construct examples that take\n1332 |     extremely long to run:\n1333 | \n1334 |     >>> from numpy.lib.stride_tricks import as_strided\n1335 |     >>> x = np.zeros([192163377], dtype=np.int8)\n1336 |     >>> x1 = as_strided(x, strides=(36674, 61119, 85569), shape=(1049, 1049, 1049))\n1337 |     >>> x2 = as