\n919 |         deg : int or 1-D array_like\n920 |             Degree(s) of the fitting polynomials. If `deg` is a single integer\n921 |             all terms up to and including the `deg`'th term are included in the\n922 |             fit. For NumPy versions >= 1.11.0 a list of integers specifying the\n923 |             degrees of the terms to include may be used instead.\n924 |         domain : {None, [beg, end], []}, optional\n925 |             Domain to use for the returned series. If ``None``,\n926 |             then a minimal domain that covers the points `x` is chosen.  If\n927 |             ``[]`` the class domain is used. The default value was the\n928 |             class domain in NumPy 1.4 and ``None`` in later versions.\n929 |             The ``[]`` option was added in numpy 1.5.0.\n930 |         rcond : float, optional\n931 |             Relative condition number of the fit. Singular values smaller\n932 |             than this relative to the largest singular value will be\n933 |             ignored. The default value is len(x)*eps, where eps is the\n934 |             relative precision of the float type, about 2e-16 in most\n935 |             cases.\n936 |         full : bool, optional\n937 |             Switch determining nature of return value. When it is False\n938 |             (the default) just the coefficients are returned, when True\n939 |             diagnostic information from the singular value decomposition is\n940 |             also returned.\n941 |         w : array_like, shape (M,), optional\n942 |             Weights. If not None the contribution of each point\n943 |             ``(x[i],y[i])`` to the fit is weighted by `w[i]`. Ideally the\n944 |             weights are chosen so that the errors of the products\n945 |             ``w[i]*y[i]`` all have the same variance.  The default value is\n946 |             None.\n947 | \n948 |             .. versionadded:: 1.5.0\n949 |         window : {[beg, end]}, optional\n950 |             Window to use for the returned series. The default\n951 |             value is the default class domain\n952 | \n953 |             .. versionadded:: 1.6.0\n954 | \n955 |         Returns\n956 |         -------\n957 |         new_series : series\n958 |             A series that represents the least squares fit to the data and\n959 |             has the domain and window specified in the call. If the\n960 |             coefficients for the unscaled and unshifted basis polynomials are\n961 |             of interest, do ``new_series.convert().coef``.\n962 | \n963 |         [resid, rank, sv, rcond] : list\n964 |             These values are only returned if `full` = True\n965 | \n966 |             resid -- sum of squared residuals of the least squares fit\n967 |             rank -- the numerical rank of the scaled Vandermonde matrix\n968 |             sv -- singular values of the scaled Vandermonde matrix\n969 |             rcond -- value of `rcond`.\n970 | \n971 |             For more details, see `linalg.lstsq`.\n972 | \n973 |         \"\"\"\n974 |         if domain is None:\n975 |             domain = pu.getdomain(x)\n976 |         elif type(domain) is list and len(domain) == 0:\n977 |             domain = cls.domain\n978 | \n979 |         if window is None:\n980 |             window = cls.window\n981 | \n982 |         xnew = pu.mapdomain(x, domain, window)\n983 |         res = cls._fit(xnew, y, deg, w=w, rcond=rcond, full=full)\n984 |         if full:\n985 |             [coef, status] = res\n986 |             return cls(coef, domain=domain, window=window), status\n987 |         else:\n988 |             coef = res\n989 |             return cls(coef, domain=domain, window=window)\n```\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"sliding window\", \"np.lib.stride_tricks.as_strided sliding window vectorized\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `numpy/core/setup_common.py:long_double_representation`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n354 | def long_double_representation(lines):\n355 |     \"\"\"Given a binary dump as given by GNU od -b, look for long double\n356 |     representation.\"\"\"\n357 | \n358 |     # Read contains a list of 32 items, each item is a byte (in octal\n359 |     # representation, as a string). We 'slide' over the output until read is of\n360 |     # the form before_seq + content + after_sequence, where content is the long double\n361 |     # representation:\n362 |     #  - content is 12 bytes: 80 bits Intel representation\n363 |     #  - content is 16 bytes: 80 bits Intel representation (64 bits) or quad precision\n364 |     #  - content is 8 bytes: same as double (not implemented yet)\n365 |     read = [''] * 32\n366 |     saw = None\n367 |     for line in lines:\n368 |         # we skip the first word, as od -b output an index at the beginning of\n369 |         # each line\n370 |         for w in line.split()[1:]:\n371 |             read.pop(0)\n372 |             read.append(w)\n373 | \n374 |             # If the end of read is equal to the after_sequence, read contains\n375 |             # the long double\n376 |             if read[-8:] == _AFTER_SEQ:\n377 |                 saw = copy.copy(read)\n378 |                 # if the content was 12 bytes, we only have 32 - 8 - 12 = 12\n379 |                 # \"before\" bytes. In other words the first 4 \"before\" bytes went\n380 |                 # past the sliding window.\n381 |                 if read[:12] == _BEFORE_SEQ[4:]:\n382 |                     if read[12:-8] == _INTEL_EXTENDED_12B:\n383 |                         return 'INTEL_EXTENDED_12_BYTES_LE'\n384 |                     if read[12:-8] == _MOTOROLA_EXTENDED_12B:\n385 |                         return 'MOTOROLA_EXTENDED_12_BYTES_BE'\n386 |                 # if the content was 16 bytes, we are left with 32-8-16 = 16\n387 |                 # \"before\" bytes, so 8 went past the sliding window.\n388 |                 elif read[:8] == _BEFORE_SEQ[8:]:\n389 |                     if read[8:-8] == _INTEL_EXTENDED_16B:\n390 |                         return 'INTEL_EXTENDED_16_BYTES_LE'\n391 |                     elif read[8:-8] == _IEEE_QUAD_PREC_BE:\n392 |                         return 'IEEE_QUAD_BE'\n393 |                     elif read[8:-8] == _IEEE_QUAD_PREC_LE:\n394 |                         return 'IEEE_QUAD_LE'\n395 |                     elif read[8:-8] == _IBM_DOUBLE_DOUBLE_LE:\n396 |                         return 'IBM_DOUBLE_DOUBLE_LE'\n397 |                     elif read[8:-8] == _IBM_DOUBLE_DOUBLE_BE:\n398 |                         return 'IBM_DOUBLE_DOUBLE_BE'\n399 |                 # if the content was 8 bytes, left with 32-8-8 = 16 bytes\n400 |                 elif read[:16] == _BEFORE_SEQ:\n401 |                     if read[16:-8] == _IEEE_DOUBLE_LE:\n402 |                         return 'IEEE_DOUBLE_LE'\n403 |                     elif read[16:-8] == _IEEE_DOUBLE_BE:\n404 |                         return 'IEEE_DOUBLE_BE'\n405 | \n406 |     if saw is not None:\n407 |         raise ValueError(\"Unrecognized format (%s)\" % saw)\n408 |     else:\n409 |         # We never detected the after_sequence\n410 |         raise ValueError(\"Could not lock sequences (%s)\" % saw)\n```\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"vectorized\", \"np.lib.stride_tricks.as_strided sliding window vectorized\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound class `numpy/lib/function_base.py:vectorize`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\nJust show the structure of this class due to response length limitations:\n```\nclass vectorize:\n    def __init__(self, pyfunc, otypes=None, doc=None, excluded=None,\n                 cache=False, signature=None):\n...\n    def __call__(self, *args, **kwargs):\n...\n    def _get_ufunc_and_otypes(self, func, args):\n...\n    def _vectorize_call(self, func, args):\n...\n    def _vectorize_call_with_signature(self, func, args):\n\n```\nHint: Search `numpy/lib/function_base.py:vectorize` to get the full content if needed.\n\n\n\n##Searching