avel`` will be returned.\n2377 |     ord : {non-zero int, inf, -inf, 'fro', 'nuc'}, optional\n2378 |         Order of the norm (see table under ``Notes``). inf means numpy's\n2379 |         `inf` object. The default is None.\n2380 |     axis : {None, int, 2-tuple of ints}, optional.\n2381 |         If `axis` is an integer, it specifies the axis of `x` along which to\n2382 |         compute the vector norms.  If `axis` is a 2-tuple, it specifies the\n2383 |         axes that hold 2-D matrices, and the matrix norms of these matrices\n2384 |         are computed.  If `axis` is None then either a vector norm (when `x`\n2385 |         is 1-D) or a matrix norm (when `x` is 2-D) is returned. The default\n2386 |         is None.\n2387 | \n2388 |         .. versionadded:: 1.8.0\n2389 | \n2390 |     keepdims : bool, optional\n2391 |         If this is set to True, the axes which are normed over are left in the\n2392 |         result as dimensions with size one.  With this option the result will\n2393 |         broadcast correctly against the original `x`.\n2394 | \n2395 |         .. versionadded:: 1.10.0\n2396 | \n2397 |     Returns\n2398 |     -------\n2399 |     n : float or ndarray\n2400 |         Norm of the matrix or vector(s).\n2401 | \n2402 |     See Also\n2403 |     --------\n2404 |     scipy.linalg.norm : Similar function in SciPy.\n2405 | \n2406 |     Notes\n2407 |     -----\n2408 |     For values of ``ord < 1``, the result is, strictly speaking, not a\n2409 |     mathematical 'norm', but it may still be useful for various numerical\n2410 |     purposes.\n2411 | \n2412 |     The following norms can be calculated:\n2413 | \n2414 |     =====  ============================  ==========================\n2415 |     ord    norm for matrices             norm for vectors\n2416 |     =====  ============================  ==========================\n2417 |     None   Frobenius norm                2-norm\n2418 |     'fro'  Frobenius norm                --\n2419 |     'nuc'  nuclear norm                  --\n2420 |     inf    max(sum(abs(x), axis=1))      max(abs(x))\n2421 |     -inf   min(sum(abs(x), axis=1))      min(abs(x))\n2422 |     0      --                            sum(x != 0)\n2423 |     1      max(sum(abs(x), axis=0))      as below\n2424 |     -1     min(sum(abs(x), axis=0))      as below\n2425 |     2      2-norm (largest sing. value)  as below\n2426 |     -2     smallest singular value       as below\n2427 |     other  --                            sum(abs(x)**ord)**(1./ord)\n2428 |     =====  ============================  ==========================\n2429 | \n2430 |     The Frobenius norm is given by [1]_:\n2431 | \n2432 |         :math:`||A||_F = [\\\\sum_{i,j} abs(a_{i,j})^2]^{1/2}`\n2433 | \n2434 |     The nuclear norm is the sum of the singular values.\n2435 | \n2436 |     Both the Frobenius and nuclear norm orders are only defined for\n2437 |     matrices and raise a ValueError when ``x.ndim != 2``.\n2438 | \n2439 |     References\n2440 |     ----------\n2441 |     .. [1] G. H. Golub and C. F. Van Loan, *Matrix Computations*,\n2442 |            Baltimore, MD, Johns Hopkins University Press, 1985, pg. 15\n2443 | \n2444 |     Examples\n2445 |     --------\n2446 |     >>> from numpy import linalg as LA\n2447 |     >>> a = np.arange(9) - 4\n2448 |     >>> a\n2449 |     array([-4, -3, -2, ...,  2,  3,  4])\n2450 |     >>> b = a.reshape((3, 3))\n2451 |     >>> b\n2452 |     array([[-4, -3, -2],\n2453 |            [-1,  0,  1],\n2454 |            [ 2,  3,  4]])\n2455 | \n2456 |     >>> LA.norm(a)\n2457 |     7.745966692414834\n2458 |     >>> LA.norm(b)\n2459 |     7.745966692414834\n2460 |     >>> LA.norm(b, 'fro')\n2461 |     7.745966692414834\n2462 |     >>> LA.norm(a, np.inf)\n2463 |     4.0\n2464 |     >>> LA.norm(b, np.inf)\n2465 |     9.0\n2466 |     >>> LA.norm(a, -np.inf)\n2467 |     0.0\n2468 |     >>> LA.norm(b, -np.inf)\n2469 |     2.0\n2470 | \n2471 |     >>> LA.norm(a, 1)\n2472 |     20.0\n2473 |     >>> LA.norm(b, 1)\n2474 |     7.0\n2475 |     >>> LA.norm(a, -1)\n2476 |     -4.6566128774142013e-010\n2477 |     >>> LA.norm(b, -1)\n2478 |     6.0\n2479 |     >>> LA.norm(a, 2)\n2480 |     7.745966692414834\n2481 |     >>> LA.norm(b, 2)\n2482 |     7.3484692283495345\n2483 | \n2484 |     >>> LA.norm(a, -2)\n2485 |     0.0\n2486 |     >>> LA.norm(b, -2)\n2487 |     1.8570331885190563e-016 # may vary\n2488 |     >>> LA.norm(a, 3)\n2489 |     5.8480354764257312 # may vary\n2490 |     >>> LA.norm(a, -3)\n2491 |     0.0\n2492 | \n2493 |     Using the `axis` argument to compute vector norms:\n2494 | \n2495 |     >>> c = np.array([[ 1, 2, 3],\n2496 |     ...               [-1, 1, 4]])\n2497 |     >>> LA.norm(c, axis=0)\n2498 |     array([ 1.41421356,  2.23606798,  5.        ])\n2499 |     >>> LA.norm(c, axis=1)\n2500 |     array([ 3.74165739,  4.24264069])\n2501 |     >>> LA.norm(c, ord=1, axis=1)\n2502 |     array([ 6.,  6.])\n2503 | \n2504 |     Using the `axis` argument to compute matrix norms:\n2505 | \n2506 |     >>> m = np.arange(8).reshape(2,2,2)\n2507 |     >>> LA.norm(m, axis=(1,2))\n2508 |     array([  3.74165739,  11.22497216])\n2509 |     >>> LA.norm(m[0, :, :]), LA.norm(m[1, :, :])\n2510 |     (3.7416573867739413, 11.224972160321824)\n2511 | \n2512 |     \"\"\"\n2513 |     x = asarray(x)\n2514 | \n2515 |     if not issubclass(x.dtype.type, (inexact, object_)):\n2516 |         x = x.astype(float)\n2517 | \n2518 |     # Immediately handle some default, simple, fast, and common cases.\n2519 |     if axis is None:\n2520 |         ndim = x.ndim\n2521 |         if ((ord is None) or\n2522 |             (ord in ('f', 'fro') and ndim == 2) or\n2523 |             (ord == 2 and ndim == 1)):\n2524 | \n2525 |             x = x.ravel(order='K')\n2526 |             if isComplexType(x.dtype.type):\n2527 |                 sqnorm = dot(x.real, x.real) + dot(x.imag, x.imag)\n2528 |             else:\n2529 |                 sqnorm = dot(x, x)\n2530 |             ret = sqrt(sqnorm)\n2531 |             if keepdims:\n2532 |                 ret = ret.reshape(ndim*[1])\n2533 |             return ret\n2534 | \n2535 |     # Normalize the `axis` argument to a tuple.\n2536 |     nd = x.ndim\n2537 |     if axis is None:\n2538 |         axis = tuple(range(nd))\n2539 |     elif not isinstance(axis, tuple):\n2540 |         try:\n2541 |             axis = int(axis)\n2542 |         except Exception as e:\n2543 |             raise TypeError(\"'axis' must be None, an integer or a tuple of integers\") from e\n2544 |         axis = (axis,)\n2545 | \n2546 |     if len(axis) == 1:\n2547 |         if ord == Inf:\n2548 |             return abs(x).max(axis=axis, keepdims=keepdims)\n2549 |         elif ord == -Inf:\n2550 |             return abs(x).min(axis=axis, keepdims=keepdims)\n2551 |         elif ord == 0:\n2552 |             # Zero norm\n2553 |             return (x != 0).astype(x.real.dtype).sum(axis=axis, keepdims=keepdims)\n2554 |         elif ord == 1:\n2555 |             # special case for speedup\n2556 |             return add.reduce(abs(x), axis=axis, keepdims=keepdims)\n2557 |         elif ord is None or ord == 2:\n2558 |             # special case for speedup\n2559 |             s = (x.conj() * x).real\n2560 |             return sqrt(add.reduce(s, axis=axis, keepdims=keepdims))\n2561 |         # None of the str-type keywords for ord ('fro', 'nuc')\n2562 |         # are valid for vectors\n2563 |         elif isinstance(ord, str):\n2564 |             raise ValueError(f\"Invalid norm order '{ord}' for vectors\")\n2565 |         else:\n2566 |             absx = abs(x)\n2567 |             absx **= ord\n2568 |             ret = add.reduce(absx, axis=axis, keepdims=keepdims)\n2569 |             ret **= (1 / ord)\n2570 |             return ret\n2571 |     elif len(axis) == 2:\n2572 |         row_axis, col_axis = axis\n2573 |         row_axis = normalize_axis_index(row_axis, nd)\n2574 |         col_axis = normalize_axis_index(col_axis, nd)\n2575 |         if row_axis == c