lti_core.append(time.time() - start)\n28 | \n29 |     plt.figure(\"scikit-learn parallel %s benchmark results\" % func.__name__)\n30 |     plt.plot(sample_sizes, one_core, label=\"one core\")\n31 |     plt.plot(sample_sizes, multi_core, label=\"multi core\")\n32 |     plt.xlabel(\"n_samples\")\n33 |     plt.ylabel(\"Time (s)\")\n34 |     plt.title(\"Parallel %s\" % func.__name__)\n35 |     plt.legend()\n```\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"calibration curve\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound .\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n  1 | \"\"\"\n  2 | ==============================\n  3 | Probability Calibration curves\n  4 | ==============================\n  5 | \n  6 | When performing classification one often wants to predict not only the class\n  7 | label, but also the associated probability. This probability gives some\n  8 | kind of confidence on the prediction. This example demonstrates how to display\n  9 | how well calibrated the predicted probabilities are and how to calibrate an\n 10 | uncalibrated classifier.\n 11 | \n 12 | The experiment is performed on an artificial dataset for binary classification\n 13 | with 100,000 samples (1,000 of them are used for model fitting) with 20\n 14 | features. Of the 20 features, only 2 are informative and 10 are redundant. The\n 15 | first figure shows the estimated probabilities obtained with logistic\n 16 | regression, Gaussian naive Bayes, and Gaussian naive Bayes with both isotonic\n 17 | calibration and sigmoid calibration. The calibration performance is evaluated\n 18 | with Brier score, reported in the legend (the smaller the better). One can\n 19 | observe here that logistic regression is well calibrated while raw Gaussian\n 20 | naive Bayes performs very badly. This is because of the redundant features\n 21 | which violate the assumption of feature-independence and result in an overly\n 22 | confident classifier, which is indicated by the typical transposed-sigmoid\n 23 | curve.\n 24 | \n 25 | Calibration of the probabilities of Gaussian naive Bayes with isotonic\n 26 | regression can fix this issue as can be seen from the nearly diagonal\n 27 | calibration curve. Sigmoid calibration also improves the brier score slightly,\n 28 | albeit not as strongly as the non-parametric isotonic regression. This can be\n 29 | attributed to the fact that we have plenty of calibration data such that the\n 30 | greater flexibility of the non-parametric model can be exploited.\n 31 | \n 32 | The second figure shows the calibration curve of a linear support-vector\n 33 | classifier (LinearSVC). LinearSVC shows the opposite behavior as Gaussian\n 34 | naive Bayes: the calibration curve has a sigmoid curve, which is typical for\n 35 | an under-confident classifier. In the case of LinearSVC, this is caused by the\n 36 | margin property of the hinge loss, which lets the model focus on hard samples\n 37 | that are close to the decision boundary (the support vectors).\n 38 | \n 39 | Both kinds of calibration can fix this issue and yield nearly identical\n 40 | results. This shows that sigmoid calibration can deal with situations where\n 41 | the calibration curve of the base classifier is sigmoid (e.g., for LinearSVC)\n 42 | but not where it is transposed-sigmoid (e.g., Gaussian naive Bayes).\n 43 | \"\"\"\n 44 | print(__doc__)\n 45 | \n 46 | # Author: Alexandre Gramfort <alexandre.gramfort@telecom-paristech.fr>\n 47 | #         Jan Hendrik Metzen <jhm@informatik.uni-bremen.de>\n 48 | # License: BSD Style.\n 49 | \n 50 | import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n```\n\nFound .\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n  1 | \"\"\"\n  2 | ========================================\n  3 | Comparison of Calibration of Classifiers\n  4 | ========================================\n  5 | \n  6 | Well calibrated classifiers are probabilistic classifiers for which the output\n  7 | of the predict_proba method can be directly interpreted as a confidence level.\n  8 | For instance a well calibrated (binary) classifier should classify the samples\n  9 | such that among the samples to which it gave a predict_proba value close to\n 10 | 0.8, approx. 80% actually belong to the positive class.\n 11 | \n 12 | LogisticRegression returns well calibrated predictions as it directly\n 13 | optimizes log-loss. In contrast, the other methods return biased probabilities,\n 14 | with different biases per method:\n 15 | \n 16 | * GaussianNaiveBayes tends to push probabilities to 0 or 1 (note the counts in\n 17 |   the histograms). This is mainly because it makes the assumption that features\n 18 |   are conditionally independent given the class, which is not the case in this\n 19 |   dataset which contains 2 redundant features.\n 20 | \n 21 | * RandomForestClassifier shows the opposite behavior: the histograms show\n 22 |   peaks at approx. 0.2 and 0.9 probability, while probabilities close to 0 or 1\n 23 |   are very rare. An explanation for this is given by Niculescu-Mizil and Caruana\n 24 |   [1]_: \"Methods such as bagging and random forests that average predictions\n 25 |   from a base set of models can have difficulty making predictions near 0 and 1\n 26 |   because variance in the underlying base models will bias predictions that\n 27 |   should be near zero or one away from these values. Because predictions are\n 28 |   restricted to the interval [0,1], errors caused by variance tend to be one-\n 29 |   sided near zero and one. For example, if a model should predict p = 0 for a\n 30 |   case, the only way bagging can achieve this is if all bagged trees predict\n 31 |   zero. If we add noise to the trees that bagging is averaging over, this noise\n 32 |   will cause some trees to predict values larger than 0 for this case, thus\n 33 |   moving the average prediction of the bagged ensemble away from 0. We observe\n 34 |   this effect most strongly with random forests because the base-level trees\n 35 |   trained with random forests have relatively high variance due to feature\n 36 |   subsetting.\" As a result, the calibration curve shows a characteristic\n 37 |   sigmoid shape, indicating that the classifier could trust its \"intuition\"\n 38 |   more and return probabilities closer to 0 or 1 typically.\n 39 | \n 40 | * Support Vector Classification (SVC) shows an even more sigmoid curve as\n 41 |   the  RandomForestClassifier, which is typical for maximum-margin methods\n 42 |   (compare Niculescu-Mizil and Caruana [1]_), which focus on hard samples\n 43 |   that are close to the decision boundary (the support vectors).\n 44 | \n 45 | .. topic:: References:\n 46 | \n 47 |     .. [1] Predicting Good Probabilities with Supervised Learning,\n 48 |           A. Niculescu-Mizil & R. Caruana, ICML 2005\n 49 | \"\"\"\n 50 | print(__doc__)\n```\n\nFound class `sklearn/calibration.py:_CalibratedClassifier`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n621 | class _CalibratedClassifier:\n622 |     \"\"\"Pipeline-like chaining a fitted classifier and its fitted calibrators.\n623 | \n624 |     Parameters\n625 |     ----------\n626 |     base_estimator : estimator instance\n627 |         Fitted classifier.\n628 | \n629 |     calibrators : list of fitted estimator instances\n630 |         List of fitted calibrators (either 'IsotonicRegression' or\n631 |         '_SigmoidCalibration'). The number of calibrators equals the number of\n632 |         classes. However, if there are 2 classes, the list contains only one\n633 |         fitted calibrator.\n634 | \n635 |     classes : array-like of shape (n_classes,)\n636 |         All the prediction classes.\n637 | \n638 |     method : {'sigmoid', 'isotonic'}, default='sigmoid'\n639 |         The method to use for calibration. Can be 'sigmoid' which\n640 |         corresponds to Platt's method or 'isotonic' which is a\n641 |         non-parametric approach based on isotonic regression.\n642 | \n643 |     Attributes\n644 |     ----------\n645 |     calibrators_ : list of fitted estimator instances\n646 |         Same as `calibrators`. Exposed for backward-compatibility. Use\n647 |         `calibrators` instead.\n648 | \n649 |         .. deprecated:: 0.24\n650 |            `calibrators_` is deprecated from 0.24 and will be removed in\n651 |            1.1 (renaming of 0.26). Use `calibrators` instead.\n652 |     \"\"\"\n653 | \n654 |     def __init__(self, base_estimator, calibrators, *, classes, method=\"sigmoid\"):\n655 |         self.base_estimator = base_estimator\n656 |         self.calibrators = calibrators\n657 |         self.classes = classes\n658 |         self.method = method\n659 | \n660 |     # TODO: Remove in 1.1\n661 |     # mypy error: Decorated property not supported\n662 |     @deprecated(  # type: ignore\n663 |         \"`calibrators_` is deprecated in 0.24 and will be removed in 1.1\"\n664 |         \"(renaming of 0.26). Use `calibrators` instead.\"\n665 |     )\n666 |     @property\n667 |     def calibrators_(self):\n668 |         return self.calibrators\n669 | \n670 |     def predict_proba(self, X):\n671 |         \"\"\"Calculate calibrated probabilities.\n672 | \n673 |         Calculates classification calibrated probabilities\n674 |         for each class, in a one-vs-all manner, for `X`.\n675 | \n676 |         Parameters\n677 |         ----------\n678 |         X : ndarray of shape (n_samples, n_features)\n679 |             The sample data.\n680 | \n681 |         Returns\n682 |         -------\n683 |         proba : array, shape (n_samples, n_classes)\n684 |             The predicted probabilities. Can be exact zeros.\n685 |         \"\"\"\n686 |         n_classes = len(self.classes)\n687 |         pred_method, method_name = _get_prediction_method(self.base_estimator)\n688 |         predictions = _compute_predictions(pred_method, method_name, X, n_classes)\n689 | \n690 |         label_encoder = LabelEncoder().fit(self.classes)\n691 |         pos_class_indices = label_encoder.transform(self.base_estimator.classes_)\n692 | \n693 |         proba = np.zeros((_num_samples(X), n_classes))\n694 |         for class_idx, this_pred, calibrator in zip(\n695 |             pos_class_indices, predictions.T, self.calibrators\n696 |         ):\n697 |             if n_classes == 2:\n698 |                 # When binary, `predictions` consists only of predictions for\n699 |                 # clf.classes_[1] but `pos_class_indices` = 0\n700 |                 class_idx += 1\n701 |             proba[:, class_idx] = calibrator.predict(this_pred)\n702 | \n703 |         # Normalize the probabilities\n704 |         if n_classes == 2:\n705 |             proba[:, 0] = 1.0 - proba[:, 1]\n706 |         else:\n707 |             denominator = np.sum(proba, axis=1)[:, np.newaxis]\n708 |             # In the edge case where for each class calibrator returns a null\n709 |             # probability for a given sample, use the uniform distribution\n710 |             # instead.\n711 |             uniform_proba = np.full_like(proba, 1 / n_classes)\n712 |             proba = np.divide(\n713 |                 proba, denominator, out=uniform_proba, where=denominator != 0\n714 |             )\n715 | \n716 |         # Deal with cases where the predicted probability minimally exceeds 1.0\n717 |         proba[(1.0 < proba) & (proba <= 1.0 + 1e-5)] = 1.0\n718 | \n719 |         return proba\n```\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"calibration curve\", \"plotting module calibration curve y_true prob_y_pred plt.gca\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `sklearn/calibration.py:_fit_calibrator`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n569 | def _fit_calibrator(clf, predictions, y, classes, method, sample_weight=None):\n570 |     \"\"\"Fit calibrator(s) and return a `_CalibratedClassifier`\n571 |     instance.\n572 | \n573 |     `n_classes` (i.e. `len(clf.classes_)`) calibrators are fitted.\n574 |     However, if `n_classes` equals 2, one calibrator is fitted.\n575 | \n576 |     Parameters\n577 |     ----------\n578 |     clf : estimator instance\n579 |         Fitted classifier.\n580 | \n581 |     predictions : array-like, shape (n_samples, n_classes) or (n_samples, 1) \\\n582 |                     when binary.\n583 |         Raw predictions returned by the un-calibrated base classifier.\n584 | \n585 |     y : array-like, shape (n_samples,)\n586 |         The targets.\n587 | \n588 |     classes : ndarray, shape (n_classes,)\n589 |         All the prediction classes.\n590 | \n591 |     method : {'sigmoid', 'isotonic'}\n592 |         The method to use for calibration.\n593 | \n594 |     sample_weight : ndarray, shape (n_samples,), default=None\n595 |         Sample weights. If None, then samples are equally weighted.\n596 | \n597 |     Returns\n598 |     -------\n599 |     pipeline : _CalibratedClassifier instance\n600 |     \"\"\"\n601 |     Y = label_binarize(y, classes=classes)\n602 |     label_encoder = LabelEncoder().fit(classes)\n603 |     pos_class_indices = label_encoder.transform(clf.classes_)\n604 |     calibrators = []\n605 |     for class_idx, this_pred in zip(pos_class_indices, predictions.T):\n606 |         if method == \"isotonic\":\n607 |             calibrator = IsotonicRegression(out_of_bounds=\"clip\")\n608 |         elif method == \"sigmoid\":\n609 |             calibrator = _SigmoidCalibration()\n610 |         else:\n611 |             raise ValueError(\n612 |                 f\"'method' should be one of: 'sigmoid' or 'isotonic'. Got {method}.\"\n613 |             )\n614 |         calibrator.fit(this_pred, Y[:, class_idx], sample_weight)\n615 |         calibrators.append(calibrator)\n616 | \n617 |     pipeline = _CalibratedClassifier(clf, calibrators, method=method, classes=classes)\n618 |     return pipeline\n```\n\nFound function `examples/calibration/plot_calibration_curve.py:plot_calibration_curve`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n 72 | def plot_calibration_curve(est, name, fig_index):\n 73 |     \"\"\"Plot calibration curve for est w/o and with calibration. \"\"\"\n 74 |     # Calibrated with isotonic calibration\n 75 |     isotonic = CalibratedClassifierCV(est, cv=2, method='isotonic')\n 76 | \n 77 |     # Calibrated with sigmoid calibration\n 78 |     sigmoid = CalibratedClassifierCV(est, cv=2, method='sigmoid')\n 79 | \n 80 |     # Logistic regression with no calibration as baseline\n 81 |     lr = LogisticRegression(C=1.)\n 82 | \n 83 |     fig = plt.figure(fig_index, figsize=(10, 10))\n 84 |     ax1 = plt.subplot2grid((3, 1), (0, 0), rowspan=2, fig=fig)\n 85 |     ax2 = plt.subplot2grid((3, 1), (2, 0), fig=fig)\n 86 | \n 87 |     ax1.plot([0, 1], [0, 1], \"k:\", label=\"Perfectly calibrated\")\n 88 |     for clf, name in [(lr, 'Logistic'),\n 89 |                       (est, name),\n 90 |                       (isotonic, name + ' + Isotonic'),\n 91 |                       (sigmoid, name + ' + Sigmoid')]:\n 92 |         clf.fit(X_train, y_train)\n 93 |         y_pred = clf.predict(X_test)\n 94 |         if hasattr(clf, \"predict_proba\"):\n 95 |             prob_pos = clf.predict_proba(X_test)[:, 1]\n 96 |         else:  # use decision function\n 97 |             prob_pos = clf.decision_function(X_test)\n 98 |             prob_pos = \\\n 99 |                 (prob_pos - prob_pos.min()) / (prob_pos.max() - prob_pos.min())\n100 | \n101 |         clf_score = brier_score_loss(y_test, prob_pos, pos_label=y.max())\n102 |         print(\"%s:\" % name)\n103 |         print(\"\\tBrier: %1.3f\" % (clf_score))\n104 |         print(\"\\tPrecision: %1.3f\" % precision_score(y_test, y_pred))\n105 |         print(\"\\tRecall: %1.3f\" % recall_score(y_test, y_pred))\n106 |         print(\"\\tF1: %1.3f\\n\" % f1_score(y_test, y_pred))\n107 | \n108 |         fraction_of_positives, mean_predicted_value = \\\n109 |             calibration_curve(y_test, prob_pos, n_bins=10)\n110 | \n111 |         ax1.plot(mean_predicted_value, fraction_of_positives, \"s-\",\n112 |       