           {\n719 |                 \"check_name\": \"this check is expected to fail because ...\",\n720 |             }\n721 | \n722 |         Where `\"check_name\"` is the name of the check, and `\"my reason\"` is why\n723 |         the check fails.\n724 | \n725 |         .. versionadded:: 1.6\n726 | \n727 |     on_skip : \"warn\", None, default=\"warn\"\n728 |         This parameter controls what happens when a check is skipped.\n729 | \n730 |         - \"warn\": A :class:`~sklearn.exceptions.SkipTestWarning` is logged\n731 |           and running tests continue.\n732 |         - None: No warning is logged and running tests continue.\n733 | \n734 |         .. versionadded:: 1.6\n735 | \n736 |     on_fail : {\"raise\", \"warn\"}, None, default=\"raise\"\n737 |         This parameter controls what happens when a check fails.\n738 | \n739 |         - \"raise\": The exception raised by the first failing check is raised and\n740 |           running tests are aborted. This does not included tests that are expected\n741 |           to fail.\n742 |         - \"warn\": A :class:`~sklearn.exceptions.EstimatorCheckFailedWarning` is logged\n743 |           and running tests continue.\n744 |         - None: No exception is raised and no warning is logged.\n745 | \n746 |         Note that if ``on_fail != \"raise\"``, no exception is raised, even if the checks\n747 |         fail. You'd need to inspect the return result of ``check_estimator`` to check\n748 |         if any checks failed.\n749 | \n750 |         .. versionadded:: 1.6\n751 | \n752 |     callback : callable, or None, default=None\n753 |         This callback will be called with the estimator and the check name,\n754 |         the exception (if any), the status of the check (xfail, failed, skipped,\n755 |         passed), and the reason for the expected failure if the check is\n756 |         expected to fail. The callable's signature needs to be::\n757 | \n758 |             def callback(\n759 |                 estim