ion should include the file path, class name (if applicable), function name, or line numbers, ordered by importance.\nYour answer would better include about 5 files.\n\nIMPORTANT FORMAT RULES:\n- Output ONLY the location block, followed by `<finish></finish>`.\n- Do NOT include explanations, bullets, headings, numbering, summaries, or notes before or after the location block.\n- Each file path MUST appear on its own line and end with `.py`.\n- Do NOT write `file.py:QualifiedName` on a single line in the final answer.\n- After a file path line, use separate `line:`, `class:`, and `function:` lines as needed.\n\n### Examples:\n```\nfull_path1/file1.py\nline: 10\nclass: MyClass1\nfunction: my_function1\n\nfull_path2/file2.py\nline: 76\nfunction: MyClass2.my_function2\n\nfull_path3/file3.py\nline: 24\nline: 156\nfunction: my_function3\n```\n\nReturn just the location(s)\n\nNote: Your thinking should be thorough and so it's fine if it's very long.\n\n--- BEGIN PROBLEM STATEMENT ---\nTitle: rewrite(abs) should call rewrite(Abs)\n\n```py\n>>> sign(x).rewrite(abs)\nsign(x)\n>>> sign(x).rewrite(Abs)\nPiecewise((0, Eq(x, 0)), (x/Abs(x), True))\n```\n\nThis is because `rewrite(x)` just dispatches to the method called `_eval_rewrite_as_x`, and the method here is called `_eval_rewrite_as_Abs` since `Abs` is the name of the class. But most people use `abs`, the built-in function which dispatches to `Abs`. So it should be special-cased in rewrite to call `Abs`.\n--- END PROBLEM STATEMENT ---\n\nIMPORTANT: You should ONLY interact with the environment provided to you AND NEVER ASK FOR HUMAN HELP.\nDon't include any lambda functions!\nYou should NOT modify any files!\n"}, {"content": "Step 1: Categorize and Extract Key Problem Information\n\nRequested feature description: `rewrite(abs)` should call `rewrite(Abs)`\nBehavior without this feature: `rewrite(abs)` does not call `rewrite(Abs)`\nExpected behavior with the feature: `rewrite(abs)` should call `rewrite(Abs)`\nAdditional context: `rewrite(x)` dispatches to `_eval_rewrite_as_x`; `Abs` is the class name and `abs` is the built-in function\n\nModules in the 'sympy' package mentioned:\n- Abs (class)\n- sign (function)\n- rewrite (method)\n\nStep 2: Locate Referenced Modules\n\nLet's start by finding the relevant modules and classes for `Abs` and `sign`.\n\n<function=search_code_snippets>\n<parameter=search_terms>\n['Abs', 'sign']\n</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "search_code_snippets", "content": "OBSERVATION:\n##Searching for term \"Abs\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound class `sympy/functions/elementary/complexes.py:Abs`.\nSource: Match found for entity name `Abs`.\nJust show the structure of this class due to response length limitations:\n```\nclass Abs(DefinedFunction):\n\n    args: tTuple[Expr]\n\n    is_extended_real = True\n    is_extended_negative = False\n    is_extended_nonnegative = True\n    unbranched = True\n    _singularities = True  # non-holomorphic\n\n    def fdiff(self, argindex=1):\n...\n    @classmethod\n    def eval(cls, arg):\n...\n    def _eval_is_real(self):\n...\n    def _eval_is_integer(self):\n...\n    def _eval_is_extended_nonzero(self):\n...\n    def _eval_is_zero(self):\n...\n    def _eval_is_extended_positive(self):\n...\n    def _eval_is_rational(self):\n...\n    def _eval_is_even(self):\n...\n    def _eval_is_odd(self):\n...\n    def _eval_is_algebraic(self):\n...\n    def _eval_power(self, exponent):\n...\n    def _eval_nseries(self, x, n, logx, cdir=0):\n...\n    def _eval_derivative(self, x):\n...\n    def _eval_rewrite_as_Heaviside(self, arg, **kwargs):\n...\n    def _eval_rewrite_as_Piecewise(self, arg, **kwargs):\n...\n    def _eval_rewrite_as_sign(self, arg, **kwargs):\n...\n    def _eval_rewrite_as_conjugate(self, arg, **kwargs):\n\n```\nHint: Search `sympy/functions/elementary/complexes.py:Abs` to get the full content if needed.\n\nFound function `symp